---
description: Best practices for TypeScript development
globs: **/*.{ts,tsx}
alwaysApply: false
---

- Prefer nullish coalescing operator (`??`) over logical OR (`||`) for default values, as `??` only falls back when values are `null` or `undefined`, while `||` falls back on any falsy value.
- Use explicit type annotations when TypeScript cannot infer types correctly.
- Avoid using `any` type; prefer `unknown` when the type is truly not known.
- Use union types (`type | type`) to represent values that could be one of several types.
- Enable strict TypeScript configuration for maximum type safety.
- Use optional chaining (`?.`) for safely accessing nested properties.
- Prefer type guards (`is` keyword) for narrowing types.
- Leverage discriminated unions for more precise type handling.
- Use the `satisfies` operator to validate object shapes while preserving literal types.
- Implement typed error handling with custom error classes.
- Avoid silent failures or empty catch blocks.
- Properly type asynchronous code including Promise return types.
- Be mindful of type complexity that might affect IDE performance.
- Use interfaces for public APIs and types for internal implementation.
- Consider using `readonly` for immutable data structures.